{"authored" [] "sources" [{"question" "q-86954" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14005246/assets/abfda82949c8ed899902e142ffab0119b734d583b299e707270e293a24a9be1b.png)\n\nFind the polar equation of the circle centered at the origin that passes through the point $A(5,2)$, as shown above." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14005246/state.json" "file_sha256" "08d422d06c1e3b8ad9d3e046024d1ea8aa41598eab1b35d07e39348cbb2a1088"} {"question" "q-86954" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "A circle of radius $k$ centered at the origin has the polar equation $r=k.$\n\nWe can compute the radius $k$ of our circle using the Pythagorean theorem, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}k & =\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{5}^{2}+{2}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{29}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the circle is $r=\\sqrt{29}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14005246/state.json" "file_sha256" "08d422d06c1e3b8ad9d3e046024d1ea8aa41598eab1b35d07e39348cbb2a1088"} {"question" "q-86954" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14005246/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "a932e73853170367a4f97c900588fc331e238762d76efe05478d3bad1c3054b2"} {"question" "q-86954" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14005246/state.json" "file_sha256" "08d422d06c1e3b8ad9d3e046024d1ea8aa41598eab1b35d07e39348cbb2a1088"} {"question" "q-86954" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "r=29"] ["math" "r=7"] ["ma